WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.350
Film Courage: Houston, what do you talk about
regarding loglines in your current book?

00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:09.670
Houston Howard, Author and Instructor: So
I always like to approach a logline of one

00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:13.720
being able to explain the story that you are
talking about.

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:17.380
The plot of your story needs to be teased
in the logline.

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:25.420
But at the same time if you can also take,
if you can also set up the larger play in

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:31.189
the logline which primarily is creating a
world and be able to communicate the story

00:00:31.189 --> 00:00:36.200
world in that logline, then not only do you
tease the plot, you also tease the world which

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:37.560
is the bigger play.

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:45.740
So the logline is just the starting point
that your project has to exist beyond the

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:46.740
logline.

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:50.730
So your logline is just one story that is
going to take place (hopefully) in this larger

00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:54.130
world where multiple stories take place and
crossover and connect in all of these interesting

00:00:54.130 --> 00:00:56.230
ways.

00:00:56.230 --> 00:01:05.080
But if you can even approach your logline
with an interesting world attached and embedded

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:12.500
in the logline, then that’s going to give
you, that’s going to intrigue people maybe

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:14.880
even more than the plot itself.

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:19.570
So if you remember The Trailer guy, a guy
name Don LaFontaine, he was “In a world…”

00:01:19.570 --> 00:01:25.110
[imitates the famous voice], he as the movie
trailer guy, he would always do those voiceovers.

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:28.680
He sort of coined that “In a world…”

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:35.500
thing and what he’s doing is “In a world…”
he sets up the context of a story world.

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:39.690
Then he’s going to tell you in the midst
of a trailer what the story is inside that

00:01:39.690 --> 00:01:40.690
world.

00:01:40.690 --> 00:01:49.659
So in a world where one day a year all crime
is legal, we have a family who is being terrorized

00:01:49.659 --> 00:01:50.670
by somebody, right?

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:54.800
So that’s how to set up THE PURGE.

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:59.900
The story world (THE PURGE) is a country where
one night year, all crime is legal.

00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:05.520
Interesting story world, interesting concept
that is far bigger than one individual story.

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:09.881
But if you can say “In a world where all
crime is legal for one night a year, then

00:02:09.881 --> 00:02:13.270
you move into your individual plot.

00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:17.151
Now you’ve intrigued me based on the plot,
but you’ve also intrigued me because there

00:02:17.151 --> 00:02:18.850
is a cool world.

00:02:18.850 --> 00:02:26.470
So it’s like an establishing shot of the
movie.

00:02:26.470 --> 00:02:32.990
So an establishing shot of the movie is if
instead of cutting directly into here where

00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:38.510
we are doing this interview we are going to
first show the outside of the entire building

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:40.810
and then movie in here.

00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:47.900
So the reason an establishing shot is a normal
thing in filmmaking is because the audience

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:53.600
is more comfortable knowing where we are then
moving into something very specific and this

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:58.250
is just the way we like (humans and audience),
this is the way we think and this is what

00:02:58.250 --> 00:03:02.780
we’re comfortable with because if you jump
directly into a scene, it can be disorienting.

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:04.770
This is why you need an establishing shot.

00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:11.930
So I look at seeding a story world inside
of a logline like having an establishing shot

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:12.930
for your logline…

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:18.130
“In a world where all crime is legal for
one day out of year…” that’s the establishing

00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:19.130
shot.

00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:22.819
Then we are jumping into the scene which is
your individual plot which you know you have

00:03:22.819 --> 00:03:31.540
a husband and wife who seek to survive the
night from a guy who breaks into their house.

00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:37.290
So it’s an interesting thing of like how
do we create big concepts from the beginning.

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:39.209
How do we create the big IP?

00:03:39.209 --> 00:03:44.690
But then ultimately we get down to the point
where we need to be able to execute and communicate

00:03:44.690 --> 00:03:46.599
it on a very small level.

00:03:46.599 --> 00:03:51.810
So how do we create STAR WARS, how do we create
something massive that can sustain for 20,

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:53.250
30, 40 years.

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:58.190
But then when I’m talking to you as a producer
or I’m at a pitchiest or whomever I’m

00:03:58.190 --> 00:04:01.240
talking to, how do I boil it down to a line?

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:04.959
Because again, we need a good story world
and we need a good story.

00:04:04.959 --> 00:04:07.120
How do I do both in a log line?

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:09.739
Typically the log line focuses on the story.

00:04:09.739 --> 00:04:11.910
But at the same time, I need the cool story
world.

00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:15.989
But I don’t want to just pitch the story
world without the story because then my product

00:04:15.989 --> 00:04:20.389
is not going to work and so I need both and
so I think you craft the logline in a really

00:04:20.389 --> 00:04:22.109
interesting way to do both.

00:04:22.109 --> 00:04:27.309
It’s just a different type of process to
approach it, but I think it’s always very

00:04:27.309 --> 00:04:27.329
helpful.

